// ===================================================================
// BASIC BROS RYDER CUP — CLOUD FIRESTORE SECURITY RULES
//
// Paste the whole contents of this file into:
//   Firebase console -> Build -> Firestore Database -> Rules tab -> Publish
//
// ---- WHY THIS FILE EXISTS ----------------------------------------
// This website does NOT use Cloud Firestore. Every piece of stored
// data — bios, scores, recaps, matchups, settings — lives in the
// Realtime Database instead (see database.rules.json). The site only
// ever loads firebase-database-compat.js; the Firestore SDK is never
// loaded and firebase.firestore() is never called.
//
// But a Firestore database still EXISTS in the Firebase project, left
// over from when the project was created. It is empty and unused, and
// it was started in "test mode", which means its rules allow anyone on
// the internet to read and write it. Google notices that and emails a
// security warning about it on a recurring basis.
//
// Locking the rules below to deny-all is what stops those emails. The
// site cannot break as a result, because nothing in the site talks to
// Firestore in the first place.
//
// (If you would rather remove the unused database entirely instead of
// locking it, that also stops the emails — see CLAUDE.md. Deleting is
// permanent, so locking it down is the safer of the two.)
//
// This file is the master copy. Nothing deploys it automatically — the
// GitHub Action only ships the website to DreamHost, it never talks to
// Firebase. These rules do nothing at all until they are pasted into
// the console and published by hand.
// ===================================================================

rules_version = '2';

service cloud.firestore {
  match /databases/{database}/documents {

    // Deny everything, to every path, for everyone.
    //
    // "{document=**}" means "any document at any depth". "if false"
    // means the request is never allowed, signed in or not. This is the
    // most locked-down rule Firestore accepts, and it is the correct
    // one here precisely because no part of this project reads or
    // writes Firestore.
    //
    // If the site is ever genuinely changed to use Firestore, replace
    // this block with real per-collection rules — do not simply loosen
    // it back to allowing everything.
    match /{document=**} {
      allow read, write: if false;
    }
  }
}
